(a) In accordance with O.C.G.A. § 38-3-56, before building, constructing, repairing, renovating or making improvements to any real property, including dwellings, homes, buildings, structures or fixtures within an area in the unincorporated area of the county designated in a declared emergency or disaster, any person, firm, partnership, corporation or other entity must register with the Crisp County Finance Department and the Crisp County Planning Department and secure a building permit and/or a regulatory permit that is posted at the work site. Each day any such entity does business in the county without complying with the ordinance from which this section was derived constitutes a separate offense.
(b) The cost of registration fees in a declared emergency or disaster is fixed at $45.00 per annum. Registration is nontransferable. The cost of the emergency building permit shall be equal to the cost for a building permit under existing regulations. The permit shall only be authorized for repairs.
(c) When registering, any person, partnership, corporation or other entity making application must, under oath, complete an application, providing the following information:
(1) Name of applicant;
(2) Permanent address and phone number of applicant;
(3) Applicant's Social Security number or federal employer identification number;
(4) If applicant is a corporation, the state and date of incorporation;
(5) Tag registration information for each vehicle to be used in the business;
(6) List of cities and/or counties where the applicant has conducted business within the past 12 months;
(7) Georgia sales tax number or authorization;
(8) Georgia business license number, if required;
(9) Copy of license from the secretary of state if required;
(10) E Verify number and date obtained and/or affidavit as required by Georgia's Security and Immigration Compliance Act.
(d) Effective date. This section shall become effective only upon the signing of a declaration of emergency by the governor and local declaration stating this section is in effect. Unless otherwise specified in the declaration of emergency or otherwise extended by the board of commissioners, the provisions of this Code section shall remain in effect during the state of emergency and for a subsequent recovery period of three months.
(Ord. of 3-26-2012, § 5)

